  "rationale": "S4 reaches the ontology through three different call shapes and the analyzer has to read all three. `setClass(\"Car\", contains = \"Vehicle\")` is inheritance: `Car` EXTENDS `Vehicle`. `setClass(\"Legacy\", representation(legacy_field = \"numeric\"))` is the OLD slot idiom -- `representation()` rather than `slots =` -- so a rule that only reads `slots` drops every pre-2000 class in the corpus, and Matrix is full of them. `setGeneric`/`setMethod`/`setValidity` are the method surface, and the pins assert `show` and `validity` as OWNED members of `Vehicle`. Two forbids matter: `Legacy` declares no base, so `extends Legacy -> Vehicle` would invent an inheritance R does not have, and `Car` inherits `Vehicle`'s slots rather than declaring them, so `contains Car -> wheels` would duplicate a fact the extends edge already carries. `speed` appears as BOTH a bare function and a Vehicle-owned method because R really does bind the name twice -- once from `setGeneric`, once from `setMethod` -- and the graph has to be able to say that.",
